---
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { lastUpdatedIso } from '../config/pages';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../config/site';
import { supportingJsonLd } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'About';
const description =
  'What this free Morse code translator is, what it does in your browser today, and who it is for. Text stays on your device; nothing you type is uploaded.';
const pageUrl = absoluteUrl('/about/');
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: absoluteUrl('/') },
  { name: 'About', url: pageUrl },
];
const jsonLd = supportingJsonLd({
  name: 'About',
  description,
  url: pageUrl,
  crumbs,
  dateModified: lastUpdatedIso('/about/'),
});
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="grid max-w-3xl gap-8 text-[1.0625rem] leading-7">
    <Breadcrumb items={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { label: 'About' }]} />
    <header class="grid gap-3">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About this Morse code translator</h1>
      <p>
        This site is a free Morse code translator that runs in your browser. It turns ordinary text into dots and dashes, and it turns Morse code back into text. You do not create an account, and the work happens on the device in front of you.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="grid gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What you can do here</h2>
      <p>
        On the home page, two linked panels keep text and Morse code in sync as you type. You can copy either panel, clear both, load a short sample, or copy a share link that keeps your message after the hash in the address. An audio row plays the Morse panel as a tone and can download the same timing as a WAV file. Speed, Farnsworth spacing, tone, and volume are controls on that row.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Listen (mic) mode lets the translator listen through your microphone and decode Morse code in real time, with all analysis running on your device.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separate pages cover the full alphabet chart, the digits 0 to 9, and the SOS distress signal. Those pages explain the same codes the translator uses, with timing notes and links back to the tool so you can hear a short example.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="grid gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where the work runs</h2>
      <p>
        Encoding, decoding, playback, and WAV building run in the browser. The text you type is not uploaded to this site for translation. A share link puts the message in the page hash, which browsers do not send to the server when they request the page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="grid gap-3">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Who it is for</h2>
      <p>
        The tool is for anyone who needs a quick, readable conversion between text and International Morse, or who wants to hear a short message at a chosen speed. Learners can use Farnsworth spacing and the alphabet pages. Operators and writers can copy Morse without installing software. It is not a radio station or an emergency service.
      </p>
      <p>
        If something on a chart looks wrong, or a control misbehaves, the
        <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href="/contact/">Contact</a>
        page explains how to write in.
        <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>
        states what the site does and does not collect.
        <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>
        cover acceptable use and limits.
        <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href="/accessibility/">Accessibility</a>
        describes keyboard and assistive-technology support.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

